Source paper: https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/physics_preliminary_examinations/response/1162943/attach/3/Twain.7z?cookie_passthrough=1

This is the ordinary sitting of 9 June 2014, whose source is retained in the archive's legacy unspecified-stage directory. All eleven questions are solved, including all four alternatives in Section B. The tutorial clarification printed with Question 9 is accounted for in <9/c>.

== 1
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Use nonrelativistic <mechanics> and let $\mathbf V=(m_1\mathbf v_1+m_2\mathbf v_2)/(m_1+m_2)$ be the constant <centre-of-mass velocity>. In the <centre-of-mass frame>, <conservation of momentum> gives $m_1\mathbf u_1+m_2\mathbf u_2=0$ before and after the <elastic collision>. Thus $\mathbf u_2=-(m_1/m_2)\mathbf u_1$, and the <kinetic energy> is
$$
T_*=\frac12m_1u_1^2+\frac12m_2u_2^2=\frac12m_1\left(1+\frac{m_1}{m_2}\right)u_1^2.
$$
The decomposition $T_{\rm lab}=T_*+\tfrac12(m_1+m_2)V^2$ and constant <centre-of-mass velocity> show that the <elastic collision> preserves $T_*$, so the positive constant multiplying $u_1^2$ forces that <speed> to remain unchanged. The same <momentum> relation fixes the second <speed>:
$$
\boxed{|\mathbf u'_1|=|\mathbf u_1|,\qquad |\mathbf u'_2|=|\mathbf u_2|.}
$$
Only their directions can change; the two <velocities> remain antiparallel in this frame. This derivation does not require a head-on <collision>.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

For equal <masses>, let the incoming <laboratory frame> <velocity> be $\mathbf v$ and the outgoing <velocities> be $\mathbf a,\mathbf b$. <Conservation of momentum> and <kinetic energy> give
$$
\mathbf v=\mathbf a+\mathbf b,\qquad v^2=a^2+b^2.
$$
Squaring the first equation and subtracting the second yields $2\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b=0$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b=0,\qquad \text{outgoing directions differ by }90^\circ.}
$$
The stated oblique <collision> ensures both outgoing <speeds> are nonzero. In the degenerate head-on case one particle can stop, and a direction cannot be assigned to its zero <velocity>.

== 2
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Let $x$ be the signed distance from the <Earth>'s centre along the polar tunnel, with $x(0)=r_0$ and $\dot x(0)=0$. The <shell theorem> leaves only the enclosed <mass> $4\pi\rho|x|^3/3$ contributing to the <gravitational field>. Consequently
$$
\ddot x=-\frac{4\pi G\rho}{3}x=-\frac{g}{r_0}x,\qquad g=\frac{4\pi G\rho r_0}{3}.
$$
This is <simple harmonic motion> with $\omega=\sqrt{g/r_0}$ and $x=r_0\cos(\omega t)$. Crossing the centre takes a quarter-period; reaching the opposite surface takes a half-period:
$$
\boxed{t_{\rm cross}=\pi\sqrt{\frac{r_0}{g}}=\sqrt{\frac{3\pi}{4G\rho}}.}
$$
The body's <mass> cancels. The polar diameter lies on the rotation axis, so the tunnel motion has no additional <centrifugal force> or <Coriolis force> from the <Earth>'s rotation. A realistic radial density profile would change the harmonic equation.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

A straight diameter at nonzero <angle> $\chi$ to the rotation axis moves the body toward that axis during the inward half of its trip. With no external axial <torque>, the total axial <angular momentum> of the <Earth> and body is conserved. The body's contribution to the <moment of inertia> decreases toward the centre, so the <Earth> spins slightly faster; the reverse happens on the outward half, restoring the original rotation rate at the far surface.

For a body of <mass> $m$ constrained to follow a tunnel fixed in the <Earth>, an illustrative angular-momentum balance is
$$
\boxed{\Omega(x)=\Omega_0\frac{I_E+mr_0^2\sin^2\chi}{I_E+mx^2\sin^2\chi}.}
$$
Here $I_E$ is the <Earth>'s axial <moment of inertia>, treated as constant, and $x$ is the signed distance along the diameter. A frictionless guide can still exert normal forces: these supply the transverse acceleration and exchange axial <angular momentum> with the rotating <Earth>. Simply allowing an unconstrained body to fall would not keep it inside a narrow nonpolar straight tunnel. The effect vanishes for $\chi=0$ and is largest for an equatorial diameter.

== 3
{scope}

=== Solution

Choose the smaller disc's initial sense as positive. For either uniform <disk>, the axial <moment of inertia> is $I=\tfrac12mr^2$, so $I_1=I_2/2$. The signed initial <angular velocities> are $\Omega_1=2\omega_2$ and $\Omega_2=-\omega_2$, giving total axial <angular momentum> zero. The frictionless axle supplies no axial <torque>, and the pins' negligible <mass> supplies no extra stored <kinetic energy>. Thus
$$
I_1\Omega'_1+I_2\Omega'_2=0,\qquad
\frac12I_1(\Omega'_1)^2+\frac12I_2(\Omega'_2)^2=\frac12I_1(2\omega_2)^2+\frac12I_2\omega_2^2.
$$
These equations allow the original state or its simultaneous reversal. A nontrivial elastic pin impact selects reversal of the relative contact <velocity>, rather than continued interpenetration. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\Omega'_1=-2\omega_2=-\omega_1,\qquad \Omega'_2=+\omega_2.}
$$
The requested magnitudes are unchanged: $|\Omega'_1|=\omega_1$ and $|\Omega'_2|=\omega_2$. The intersecting pin paths make the impact possible; no additional lever arm is needed once axial <angular momentum> and <energy> conservation have been imposed.

== 4
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

<Generalized coordinates> are variables $q_1,\ldots,q_n$ that specify the allowed instantaneous configuration of a <mechanical system>. They need not be Cartesian positions: a rigid pendulum's <angle> is a suitable single coordinate. With constraints solved, the particle positions are functions $\mathbf r_i(q_1,\ldots,q_n,t)$.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

The number of <degrees of freedom> is the number of independent coordinates needed to specify an allowed configuration locally. For $N$ unconstrained point particles in three dimensions it is $3N$; $k$ independent regular <holonomic constraints> reduce it to $3N-k$. Redundant constraints must not be counted twice, and nonholonomic velocity restrictions require separate care.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

A <holonomic constraint> can be written as an equation $f(q_1,\ldots,q_n,t)=0$ involving coordinates and possibly time, without an irreducible dependence on <velocities>. For example a particle confined to a fixed sphere obeys $x^2+y^2+z^2-a^2=0$. A general <rolling without slipping> constraint in three dimensions need not be integrable into such a coordinate equation.

== 5
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Use the inverse <Lorentz transformation>, with $\gamma_u=(1-u^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$:
$$
x=\gamma_u(x'+ut'),\qquad t=\gamma_u\left(t'+\frac{ux'}{c^2}\right).
$$
Taking differentials along the object's trajectory gives
$$
\boxed{w=\frac{dx}{dt}=\frac{v+u}{1+uv/c^2}.}
$$
This is the collinear <relativistic velocity addition> law. It concerns the <velocity> of one object measured in two frames. By contrast the rate of change of the distance between two objects in one <laboratory frame> can be as large as
$$
\boxed{\frac{dD}{dt}=2c,}
$$
attained by oppositely directed light signals; for massive objects it is approached but not attained. This is not an object's <speed> in the other's rest frame and cannot transmit a signal faster than $c$.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

Take the light to travel along positive x, while the water moves with signed <velocity> $-v$. In the water's rest frame its light <phase velocity> is $c/n$. The <relativistic velocity addition> law from <5/a> gives the laboratory <phase velocity>
$$
w=\frac{c/n-v}{1-v/(nc)},\qquad
n'=\frac cw=n\frac{1-v/(nc)}{1-nv/c}.
$$
Expanding to first order in $v/c$, with $nv/c\ll1$, yields
$$
\boxed{n'\simeq n\left(1+\frac{nv}{c}-\frac{v}{nc}\right).}
$$
Equivalently $w\simeq c/n-v(1-1/n^2)$: the counterflow reduces the forward light <speed>. This is the nondispersive <Fresnel drag coefficient>. The <refractive index> is taken as constant over the relevant frequencies; <dispersion> and its frequency-shift corrections are outside this approximation.

== 6
{scope}

=== Solution

For $\beta=0.99$, the <Lorentz factor> is $\gamma=1/\sqrt{1-\beta^2}=7.0888$. In the tracks' rest frame the train undergoes <length contraction> to $100/\gamma=14.1067\,\mathrm m$, which fits in the $90\,\mathrm m$ tunnel. There is a time interval during which it is entirely inside. In the train's rest frame the tunnel instead has length $90/\gamma=12.6961\,\mathrm m$, so it never simultaneously contains the whole $100\,\mathrm m$ train:
$$
\boxed{\text{Entirely inside at one track-frame time: yes. At one train-frame time: no.}}
$$
These refer to different sets of simultaneous events, as required by <relativity of simultaneity>. Quantitatively let $A$ be the rear entering the near portal, with $(x_A,t_A)=(0,0)$, and $B$ the front reaching the far portal. In the track frame,
$$
x_B=90\,\mathrm m,\qquad ct_B=\frac{90-100/\gamma}{\beta}=76.6599\,\mathrm m.
$$
The train is wholly inside between these events. Transforming their time separation gives
$$
c(t'_B-t'_A)=\gamma(ct_B-\beta x_B)=-88.1858\,\mathrm m<0.
$$
Thus the front leaves before the rear enters in the train frame. The two events are <spacelike separation>[spacelike separated], so their time order may reverse without a causal contradiction. No doors or forced acceleration need be added to the problem.

\Image[2014-train-and-tunnel-events.png]
{title=Portal and train <worldlines> in the two inertial frames}
{description=Original <spacetime diagrams>, with vertical coordinates ct and ct-prime in metres. Events A and B exchange time order; the horizontal shaded time slice shows the different simultaneous configurations.}

== 7
{scope}

=== Solution

Let $\theta$ measure the spoke's displacement from downward equilibrium. The total <moment of inertia> about the axle is $I=MR_2^2+mR_1^2$: the rim is a thin <thin hoop>, and the attached body is a point <mass>. The rim's weight has no axle <torque>, while the attached <mass> gives restoring <torque> $-mgR_1\sin\theta$. Hence
$$
\boxed{(MR_2^2+mR_1^2)\ddot\theta+mgR_1\sin\theta=0,\quad \theta(0)=\theta_0,\quad\dot\theta(0)=0.}
$$
Equivalently <conservation of energy> gives $\tfrac12I\dot\theta^2=mgR_1(\cos\theta-\cos\theta_0)$. For small <angles>, $\sin\theta\simeq\theta$, so the motion is approximately $\theta=\theta_0\cos(\omega t)$ with $\omega^2=mgR_1/I$. The small-amplitude period is
$$
\boxed{T=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{MR_2^2+mR_1^2}{mgR_1}}.}
$$
At finite amplitude the nonlinear pendulum equation remains valid but this period approximation does not. The $M\to0$ limit recovers a simple pendulum of length $R_1$.

== 8
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Choose positive x along the rocket's motion. If its instantaneous total <mass> is $\mu$ and it ejects a positive amount $d\eta=-d\mu$, the exhaust <velocity> in the original <inertial frame> is $v-u_0$ to first order. <Conservation of momentum> over that increment gives
$$
\mu v=(\mu-d\eta)(v+dv)+d\eta(v-u_0),\qquad \mu\,dv=u_0d\eta=-u_0d\mu.
$$
Integrating the <rocket equation> from $\mu=m+M$ to $\mu=m$ gives
$$
\boxed{v_f=u_0\ln\frac{m+M}{m}.}
$$
The constant exhaust <speed> is relative to the rocket, not the initial frame. <Kinetic energy> of rocket plus exhaust is not conserved because stored fuel <energy> is released. The calculation is Newtonian and requires resulting <speeds> small compared with the <speed of light>.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

Let $q$ be the transferred fuel <mass>. Before any engine reversal, rocket A has <mass> $a=m_A+M-q$ and <velocity>
$$
v_A(q)=u_0\ln\frac{m_A+M}{m_A+M-q}.
$$
For <exhaust collection by a second rocket>, under the stipulated eventual capture of all exhaust, final <conservation of momentum> for the complete initially stationary system gives $m_Av_{Af}+(m_B+M)v_{Bf}=0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{v_{Af}=u_0\ln\frac{m_A+M}{m_A},\quad v_{Bf}=-\frac{m_A}{m_B+M}v_{Af},\quad v_{\rm rel}=u_0\frac{m_A+m_B+M}{m_B+M}\ln\frac{m_A+M}{m_A}.}
$$
Capture is inelastic: no <kinetic energy> conservation for B is assumed. Finite exhaust flight time changes the intermediate sharing of <momentum> but not this endpoint balance if the stated complete capture actually occurs.

The <ballistic exhaust interception condition> is a physical restriction on that idealization. For purely ballistic exhaust to reach a receding collector to the rear, its laboratory <velocity> must be more negative than B's while interception occurs. In the negligible-flight-time approximation this requires $v_A-u_0<v_B$. Very large fuel ratios need not meet that condition throughout a complete burn; the boxed endpoint formula is conditional on the prompt's complete-collection stipulation, not an unconditional prediction for every ballistic two-rocket geometry.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

Neglecting exhaust flight time, no <momentum> is stored in transit and
$$
(m_A+M-q)v_A+(m_B+q)v_B=0.
$$
Since the velocities have opposite signs, equal nonzero <speeds> imply equality of the instantaneous <masses>. For $m_B=2m_A$ and $M=5m_A$, this occurs at $6m_A-q=2m_A+q$, or $q=2m_A$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{v_s=u_0\ln\frac{6m_A}{4m_A}=u_0\ln\frac32.}
$$
The common-speed event occurs after $40\%$ of A's original fuel has been transferred, rather than when either rocket has its final <mass>. At this event the rearward exhaust still overtakes B: $v_A-u_0=-0.5945u_0<v_B=-0.4055u_0$. Thus the interception restriction in <8/b> does not invalidate this event even though extending complete ballistic capture over the much later burn would need separate checking.

=== d
{scope}

==== Solution

Put $m_A=M=m$ and $m_B=2m$. At reversal the transferred <mass> is $q_r=0.45m$, leaving A with $1.55m$ and, after capture of that exhaust, B with $2.45m$. The first burn gives
$$
v_{Ar}=u_0\ln\frac2{1.55}=u_0\ln\frac{40}{31},\qquad
v_{Br}=-\frac{1.55}{2.45}v_{Ar}=-\frac{31}{49}u_0\ln\frac{40}{31}.
$$
After reversal A emits its remaining exhaust away from B, so B receives no further fuel and eventually coasts at this <velocity>. Applying the <rocket equation> with reversed thrust to A gives
$$
v_{Af}=v_{Ar}-u_0\ln1.55=u_0\ln\frac{800}{961}\simeq-0.18336u_0,\qquad v_{Bf}\simeq-0.16126u_0.
$$
Both now move toward negative x, but A moves faster in that direction. The asymptotic closing <speed> is
$$
\boxed{v_{Bf}-v_{Af}\simeq0.0221u_0>0:\quad\text{A can catch B.}}
$$
This conclusion does not require a burn rate or an initial separation: after a finite burn, the positive eventual closing <speed> closes any finite remaining gap. Those additional inputs would be required to calculate the meeting time. Any pre-reversal exhaust still in transit affects the timing, not B's eventual <momentum> after collecting that fixed amount.

\Image[2014-exhaust-collection-speeds.png]
{title=Signed <velocities> during exhaust collection and thrust reversal}
{description=Original Newtonian calculations versus the fraction of original fuel consumed. Left: the equal-speed event in the ideal negligible-flight-time collection model; the later dashed extension is conditional on collection and can violate ballistic interception. Right: reversing thrust at 45 percent produces a small positive eventual closing speed.}

== 9
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

For the <eccentric circular-hole disk inertia>, let $D=\pi\rho R^2$ be the <mass> of the complete disc before removing material, $h=\pi\rho r_o^2$ the missing <mass>, and $W=D-h$ the wheel's remaining <mass>. Denote the complete disc's centre by C and the axle/hole centre by H; $CH=r_o$. Assume the hole fits inside the disc, $0<r_o<R/2$. The <parallel axis theorem> gives the full disc's <moment of inertia> about H as $\tfrac12DR^2+Dr_o^2$. Subtract the absent little disc about its own centre H:
$$
\boxed{I_H=\frac12DR^2+Dr_o^2-\frac12hr_o^2=\frac{\pi\rho}{2}\left(R^4+2R^2r_o^2-r_o^4\right).}
$$
The axle is a support, not part of the rotating wheel's stated <mass>. Its frictionless bearing does not alter this <moment of inertia>. For fixed-axis <angular velocity> $\omega$,
$$
\boxed{T_H=\frac12I_H\omega^2=\frac{\pi\rho\omega^2}{4}\left(R^4+2R^2r_o^2-r_o^4\right).}
$$
The missing material must be subtracted with its actual displacement relative to the axis; treating the wheel as a concentric annulus would give the wrong result.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

For a freely rolling rigid body, decompose the <kinetic energy> into translation of its <centre of mass> G and rotation about G. The <centre of mass after removing material> lies on the side opposite H:
$$
\overrightarrow{CG}=-\frac hW\overrightarrow{CH},\qquad HG=r_o\left(1+\frac hW\right)=\frac{Dr_o}{W}.
$$
Another use of the <parallel axis theorem> gives
$$
I_G=I_H-W(HG)^2=I_H-\frac{D^2r_o^2}{W}.
$$
Thus at the same <angular speed>,
$$
\boxed{T_{\rm rot,G}=\frac12I_G\omega^2<T_H,\qquad T_H-T_{\rm rot,G}=\frac{D^2r_o^2\omega^2}{2W}>0.}
$$
This compares the rotational component, not the total rolling <kinetic energy>. The total also contains $\tfrac12Wv_G^2$ and need not be smaller than $T_H$. Describing all rolling motion as instantaneous rotation about its contact point includes that translational contribution; it is not the rotational component intended by this comparison.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

The supplied PDF labels this equation *clarified from original for tutorial purposes*. Use the model of an <eccentric wheel with missing-mass kinetic energy neglected>: neglect the missing disc's contribution to <kinetic energy>, while retaining its subtraction from gravitational <potential energy>. Do not silently replace the gravitational <mass> $W$ by the full-disc <mass> $D$ as well.

Choose $\theta$ as the clockwise <angle> of $\overrightarrow{CH}$ from the upward vertical, and set $s=R\theta$. The <rolling without slipping> constraint makes changes in $s$ equal changes in the distance C travels down the slope. Choose its additive origin so $s=0$ when the hole is vertically above C. Then, up to an irrelevant height constant,
$$
y_C=-s\sin\alpha+\mathrm{constant},\qquad y_H=y_C+r_o\cos(s/R).
$$
The approximate <kinetic energy> is that of the complete uniform disc rolling at $\dot s=R\dot\theta$:
$$
T\simeq\frac12D\dot s^2+\frac12\left(\frac12DR^2\right)\frac{\dot s^2}{R^2}=\frac34D\dot s^2.
$$
Subtracting the missing disc's gravitational <potential energy> gives
$$
U=Dgy_C-hgy_H=-Wgs\sin\alpha-hgr_o\cos(s/R)+\mathrm{constant}.
$$
Apply the <Euler-Lagrange equation> to $\mathcal L=T-U$:
$$
\frac32D\ddot s+\frac{hgr_o}{R}\sin(s/R)-Wg\sin\alpha=0.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{K_1=\frac{2hgr_o}{3DR}=\frac{2gr_o^3}{3R^3},\qquad K_2=-\frac{2Wg\sin\alpha}{3D}=-\frac{2g}{3}\left(1-\frac{r_o^2}{R^2}\right)\sin\alpha,\qquad\gamma=\frac1R.}
$$
Here $K_1,K_2$ have dimensions of <acceleration> and $\gamma$ has dimensions of inverse <length>. The initial conditions are $s(0)=\dot s(0)=0$ under the vertical-above interpretation. If an initial orientation is instead specified relative to the slope normal, the same coordinate convention produces a different initial $s$, not different constants. With $r_o\to0$ the equation becomes $\ddot s=(2/3)g\sin\alpha$, the familiar solid-disc result. Retaining the hole's exact <kinetic energy> would introduce coordinate-dependent inertia and extra $\dot s^2$ terms; the printed neglect is what permits the requested constant-coefficient form.

\Image[2014-perforated-wheel-geometry.png]
{title=The axle, geometric centre and <centre of mass> of the perforated wheel}
{description=Original geometric constructions. Left: fixed-axis inertia subtracts a missing disc about H. Right: the rolling coordinate is the hole angle from the upward vertical; the centre travels downhill by R times the angle, while the missing mass contributes a cosine to potential energy.}

== 10
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

The incident particle obeys the <energy-momentum relation> $E^2=p^2c^2+m_1^2c^4$. The stationary target contributes <energy> $m_2c^2$ and zero <momentum>. A single product inherits the total <four-momentum>, so its <invariant mass> satisfies
$$
M^2c^4=(E+m_2c^2)^2-p^2c^2=m_1^2c^4+m_2^2c^4+2Em_2c^2.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{M=\sqrt{m_1^2+m_2^2+\frac{2m_2E}{c^2}}.}
$$
Here $E$ is the incident total <energy>, including rest <energy>. In a one-particle final state, exact production of a specified <mass> fixes the incident <energy>; there is no adjustable final relative motion to absorb excess <energy>. A resonance with a finite <decay width> relaxes that ideal exact equality over its line shape.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

For fixed-target <electron>-<positron> formation, set $m_1=m_2=m_e$ in <10/a> and solve for the incident total <energy>:
$$
\boxed{E_{e^+}=\frac{m_\rho^2c^4-2m_e^2c^4}{2m_ec^2}=\frac{(768\,\mathrm{MeV})^2}{2(0.511\,\mathrm{MeV})}-0.511\,\mathrm{MeV}\simeq5.7713\times10^5\,\mathrm{MeV}=577.1\,\mathrm{GeV}.}
$$
The incident <kinetic energy> is smaller by another $m_ec^2$, a negligible relative change here. Most of the fixed-target <energy> becomes motion of the final <rho meson> in the laboratory.

With equal and opposite incoming <momenta>, the <centre-of-momentum frame> is the laboratory. Each incoming particle has the same total <energy>, and there is no final translational <kinetic energy> at formation:
$$
\boxed{E_{e^+}=E_{e^-}=\frac{m_\rho c^2}{2}=384\,\mathrm{MeV}.}
$$
This large contrast illustrates the invariant-energy advantage of colliding beams. The given $768\,\mathrm{MeV}/c^2$ resonance <mass> is used rather than replacing it with a modern tabulated value.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

Treat the atmospheric <proton> as stationary in the <Earth> frame, the incident <antiproton> as vertically downward, and neglect atmospheric <energy loss>, scattering and the <Earth>'s curvature initially. Take $m_pc^2=0.9383\,\mathrm{GeV}$. <Conservation of energy> and equal outgoing <energies> give
$$
E_\mu=\frac{35+0.9383}{2}\,\mathrm{GeV}=17.9692\,\mathrm{GeV},\qquad \gamma_\mu=\frac{E_\mu}{0.106\,\mathrm{GeV}}\simeq169.52.
$$
Equal <energies> imply equal <momentum> magnitudes. Their transverse <momenta> cancel, so the two <muons> have equal downward components $p_z=p_{\bar p}/2$, where
$$
p_{\bar p}c=\sqrt{(35\,\mathrm{GeV})^2-(0.9383\,\mathrm{GeV})^2}\simeq34.9874\,\mathrm{GeV}.
$$
Each <muon> therefore makes an <angle> $\theta$ with the downward vertical satisfying
$$
\cos\theta=\frac{p_{\bar p}c/2}{\sqrt{E_\mu^2-m_\mu^2c^4}}\simeq0.9735.
$$
The paths are not both vertical: replacing their path lengths by $20\,\mathrm{km}$ would miss this two-body kinematic correction. Since $v_z=p_zc^2/E_\mu$, the proper flight time over vertical height $H$ is
$$
\Delta\tau_\mu=\frac{H}{\gamma_\mu v_z}=\frac{2Hm_\mu c^2}{c(p_{\bar p}c)}.
$$
For <equal-energy atmospheric muon-pair survival>, independent exponential <particle decays> with mean proper <muon lifetime> $\tau_0=2.2\,\mu\mathrm s$, both survive with <probability>
$$
\boxed{P_{\rm both}=\exp\left[-\frac{4Hm_\mu c^2}{c\tau_0(p_{\bar p}c)}\right]\simeq0.692.}
$$
Equivalently each travels about $20.55\,\mathrm{km}$, with proper flight time about $0.404\,\mu\mathrm s$, and survives with <probability> about $0.832$. The result is decay-only survival to an ideal surface, not a prediction including air interactions or detector efficiency. For a spherical <Earth> of radius $R_E$, the exact path length is $\ell=(R_E+H)\cos\theta-\sqrt{R_E^2-(R_E+H)^2\sin^2\theta}$; inserting $P_{\rm both}=\exp[-2\ell/(\gamma_\mu v_\mu\tau_0)]$ gives a small curvature correction.

== 11
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Here $G$ is the <gravitational constant>, $r$ is the separation of the orbiting object from the central body's centre, and $J$ is the magnitude of the object's conserved <orbital angular momentum>, not angular momentum per unit <mass>. Approximate the much heavier central body as fixed, neglect other forces, and use the Newtonian gravitational <potential energy> $U=-GMm_0/r$, with zero at infinity.

A <central force> confines the motion to a fixed <orbital plane>. In plane polar coordinates its <kinetic energy> is $T=\tfrac12m_0(\dot r^2+r^2\dot\phi^2)$. Conservation of <angular momentum> gives $J=m_0r^2\dot\phi$; eliminating $\dot\phi$ yields
$$
\boxed{E=\frac12m_0\dot r^2+V_{\rm eff}(r),\qquad V_{\rm eff}(r)=\frac{J^2}{2m_0r^2}-\frac{GMm_0}{r}.}
$$
The centrifugal term belongs to the radial <effective potential>; it is the eliminated transverse <kinetic energy>, not an additional physical interaction. If recoil of the central body mattered, the relative-coordinate reduction would use the <reduced mass>.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

Write $\mu=GM$ and let $v_c=\sqrt{\mu/r_0}$ be the original circular <orbital speed>. An impulse leaves the <orbital angular momentum> unchanged if it is radial: its <angular impulse> $\mathbf r\times\Delta\mathbf p$ is zero. The tangential <velocity> remains $v_c$, and an added radial component $w$ gives
$$
v_c^2+w^2=\beta v_c^2,\qquad \boxed{\Delta\mathbf v=\pm v_c\sqrt{\beta-1}\,\hat{\mathbf r}.}
$$
Thus <kinetic energy> increases even though <angular momentum> does not. For a <radial kick from a circular Kepler orbit>, the new specific <orbital energy> and squared specific <angular momentum> are
$$
\varepsilon=\frac{\mu}{2r_0}(\beta-2),\qquad j^2=\mu r_0.
$$
The <orbital eccentricity> is $e=\sqrt{1+2\varepsilon j^2/\mu^2}=\sqrt{\beta-1}$, while the <semi-latus rectum> is $p=j^2/\mu=r_0$. Hence
$$
\boxed{r_{\min}=\frac{r_0}{1+\sqrt{\beta-1}},\qquad r_{\max}=\frac{r_0}{1-\sqrt{\beta-1}}\quad(1<\beta<2).}
$$
For $\beta=2$ the trajectory is a <parabolic Kepler orbit> and has no finite furthest radius; its periapsis is $r_0/2$. For $\beta>2$ it is a <hyperbolic orbit>, with the same formula for $r_{\min}$ but unbounded radius:
$$
\boxed{r_{\max}=\infty\quad\text{for }\beta\geq2.}
$$
One must not use the negative denominator in the bound-orbit expression as a negative physical apocentre. On a departing unbound branch the conic's periapsis may already lie in the past; the future minimum from the kick event is then $r_0$. These are ideal conic orbits; a periapsis inside a real planet would cause impact before the ideal continuation.

\Image[2014-radial-kick-and-launch-error-orbits.png]
{title=<Radial impulse>, orbital turning radii and a launch-direction error}
{description=Original dimensionless orbit calculations. Left: a radial kick changes energy while preserving tangential velocity and angular momentum. Middle: fixed-angular-momentum effective potential with bound, marginal and escaping energies. Right: a fixed-speed direction error gives an ellipse with the intended circular orbit's semimajor axis.}

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

For a <fixed-speed orbital launch-direction error>, the initial radius and <speed> are correct, so the specific <orbital energy> is still $\varepsilon=-GM/(2R)$ and the new <semi-major axis> is still $a=R$. Resolve the launch <velocity> into radial and tangential components, taking $\alpha$ as the in-plane deviation from the intended tangent:
$$
v_r=\sqrt{\frac{GM}{R}}\sin\alpha,\qquad v_\phi=\sqrt{\frac{GM}{R}}\cos\alpha,\qquad j=\sqrt{GMR}\cos\alpha.
$$
For a nondegenerate orbit $\cos\alpha\ne0$, the <orbital eccentricity> follows from $j^2=GMa(1-e^2)$:
$$
e=|\sin\alpha|,\qquad r_{\min}=R(1-|\sin\alpha|),\qquad r_{\max}=R(1+|\sin\alpha|).
$$
The intended circular orbit has radius $R$ everywhere, so the largest radial distance between the orbit curves occurs at either apsis:
$$
\boxed{\Delta r_{\max}=R|\sin\alpha|\simeq R|\alpha|\quad\text{for small }|\alpha|\text{ in radians}.}
$$
This is the geometric separation of the two orbit curves, not the distance between two satellites compared at equal elapsed times. The latter also depends on orbital phase. A purely radial launch is a degenerate zero-angular-momentum trajectory through the central body and requires separate treatment of the collision.
